+\paragraph{Implications for multi-view texturing}
+The evidence separates control tuning from appearance validation. First, Fixed High and Generic Linear are essential comparators: a gain over Fixed Low alone does not establish that a temporal schedule improves the available inference controls. Second, the four equal-budget GeoTex controls compare complete allocation policies, so their measured differences remain informative even though the evolving residuals prevent a further mechanism attribution. Third, image-space agreement cannot replace checking projected appearance: broad image/bake rank association coexists with a different LPIPS winner on seven of ten objects, and GT-256 reveals larger reference-appearance errors than the differences between schedules. These observations support measuring both domains with strong controls; they do not supply a universally preferred deployment setting. Native MV-Adapter results delimit the interpretation to the tested interface rather than extending GeoTex's numerical scales or preferred window across systems.
